nfavorable   Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  04 REMOVED_MONTH 98 at 11 32     ADDITIONAL DIAGNOSTIC INFORMATION Addendum     NOTE  This case is re examined after a review by pathologists at the Brigham and Women s Hospital arrived at greater lymph node count  with implications for subsequent therapy  In the right axillary node dissection  a subcapsular micrometastasis is noted in a second lymph node that was missed on initial exam  In the mastectomy specimen  one rounded nodule appears to represent replacement of an intramammary node  A   another nodule may be a replaced node  but the evidence is less definite  E   Lympho  vascular invasion is present  and many areas show a prominent lymphoid response  These findings were discussed with Dr  Barbara Smith  Kevin Camphausen and Judy Garber  Added by  John H  Eichhorn  M D  Signed On  22 REMOVED_MONTH 99 at 23 01 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  right breast  is a mastectomy specimen measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 3 5cm with an attached ellipse of skin including the nipple measuring REMOVED_DATE x 2 0cm  Sections reveal multiple poorly circumscribed white fibrotic nodules consistent grossly with invasive carcinoma  ranging in size from REMOVED_DATE to up to 2 5cm  Two of the nodules are located medially extending to up to 1 0cm from the medial resection margin  One of the nodules extends to less than 0 5cm from the deep margin  Additionally there is a poorly defined fibrotic area extending from the nipple towards the lateral aspect measuring REMOVED_DATE x 7 0cm abutting the deep margin  Sections through the areola region reveals multiple dilated ducts filled with yellow thick material  Sections taken as follows  Section code  A  Small nodules  one of them to deep margin   B E  Representative sections of different nodules  F  Subareolar region  G H  Poorly defined fibrotic area  to deep margin   Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_